// Reversing motor starter, after IEC 60204-1 (electrical equipment of
// machines): the forward and reverse contactors are interlocked, so both
// can never be closed -- that is a short across two phases -- and a change
// of direction goes through stop, with a dead time for the arc in the
// opening contactor to clear before the other one closes.
//
// The standard wants the interlock in the wiring as well (auxiliary NC
// contacts, often a mechanical one); this is the control side of it.
//
// Pins are the board's business.

#digital  BtnFwd in 1
#digital  BtnRev in 2
#digital  BtnStop in 3
#digital  KFwd out 4                // forward contactor
#digital  KRev out 5                // reverse contactor
#timer    Dead 200                  // ms between one opening, the other closing
#variable Wait:1 = 0                // the dead time is running

#states stopped fwd rev

#annotate varp invariant "!(KFwd && KRev)"
#annotate varp invariant "!(State == stopped && (KFwd || KRev))"
// Each contactor is closed only in its own state -- the two above follow,
// and these are what make them provable by induction:
#annotate varp invariant "!KFwd || State == fwd"
#annotate varp invariant "!KRev || State == rev"
#annotate varp reach "State == stopped && State' == rev"
// Goals never reached, for the solver:
//     Wait && State' != stopped               the dead time is waited out
//     State == fwd && State' == rev           no direct reversal
//     State == rev && State' == fwd

#in INIT
    State = stopped
#end

#in stopped
    State = fwd, KFwd = 1 ? !Wait && BtnFwd && !BtnRev && !BtnStop
    State = rev, KRev = 1 ? !Wait && BtnRev && !BtnFwd && !BtnStop
    Wait = 0 ? timeout(Dead)
#end

// Stop, or the other direction asked for: open, and wait out the dead time.
#in fwd
    State = stopped, KFwd = 0, Dead = 1, Wait = 1 ? BtnStop || BtnRev
#end
#in rev
    State = stopped, KRev = 0, Dead = 1, Wait = 1 ? BtnStop || BtnFwd
#end
